\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 11: More Topics in Linear Unobserved Effects Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Hausman-Taylor Model, Sequential Exogeneity, Dynamic Panel Models, Unbalanced
 Panels
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.1 GMM for RE, FE, and FD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We extensively covered estimation of the unobserved effects model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which we can write for all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}=x_{i}\beta+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of ones 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of idiosyncratic errors, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of composite errors.
 With strictly exogeneous explanatory variables, RE, FE, and FD are still
 the most popular approaches to estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in equation (2).
 Each of these estimators is consistent without restrictions on the variance-cov
ariance matrix of the composite or idiosyncratic errors.
 We also saw that each estimator is asymptotically efficient under a particular
 set of assumptions on the conditional second moment matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the RE case, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the FE case, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the FD case.
 Recall that there are two aspects to the second moment assumptions that
 imply asymptotic efficiency.
 First, the unconditional variance-covariance matrix is assumed to have
 a special structure.
 Second, system homoskedasticity is assumed in all cases.
 (Loosely, conditional variances and covariances do not depend on the explanator
y variables.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have already seen how to allow for an unrestricted unconditional variance-cov
ariance matrix in the context of RE, FE, and FD: we simply apply unrestriced
 FGLS to the appropriately transformed equations.
 Nevertheless, efficiency of, say, the FEGLS estimators hinge on the system
 homoskedasticity assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Under RE.1, efficiency of the FGLS estimator with unrestricted 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is ensured only if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 If this assumption fails, GMM with an optimal weighting matrix is generally
 more efficient, and so it may be worthwhile to apply GMM methods to (2),
 or in the case of FE and FD, a suitably transformed set of equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.3 Hausman and Taylor-Type Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The results of the previous seciton apply to a class of unobserved effects
 models studied by Hausman and Taylor (1981) (HT).
 The key feature of these models is that the assumptions imply the availability
 of instrumental variables form within the model; one need not look outside
 the model for exogenous variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The HT model can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=w_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 display some time variation, and it is convient to include unity in 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 We assume strict exogeneity conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 can proceed by FE.
 The FE transformation eliminates 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 As usual, this approach places nor estrictions on the correlation between
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(w_{i},x_{it})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What about estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

? If, in addition to (11), we assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(w_{i}c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

then a 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent estimator is easy to obtain: average equation (1) across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, premulitply by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, take expectations, use the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[w_{i}\left(c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}\right)\right]=0$
\end_inset

, and rearrange to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(w_{i}w_{i}^{\prime})\gamma=E\left[w_{i}(\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, making the standard assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}w_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, it follows by the usual analogy principle argument that
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\gamma}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}w_{i}w_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}\left(\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}\right)}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

.
 The asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\gamma}-\gamma)$
\end_inset

 can be obtained by standard arguments for two-step estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hausman and Taylor (1981) partition 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=(w_{i1},w_{i2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}=(x_{it1},x_{it2})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

, and assume that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(w_{i1}c_{i})=0\ \mbox{and}\ E(x_{it1}c_{i})=0,\ \forall\ t
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 We still maintain assumption (4), so that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumptions (4) and (8) provide orthogonality conditions that can be used
 in a method of moments procedure.
 Write equation (3) for all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=w_{i}\gamma+x_{i}\beta+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the composite error.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is strictly exogenous and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}v_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

 [where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}\equiv I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is again the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 time-demeaning matrix], it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E[(Q_{T}x_{i})^{\prime}v_{i}]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be used as instruments in estimation equation (16).
 If these were the only instruments available, then we would be back to
 FE estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 without being able to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Additional instruments come from assumption (8).
 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is orthogonal to 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and so is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times TK_{1}$
\end_inset

 vector containing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, define a set of instruments for equation (9) by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[Q_{T}x_{i},j_{T}\otimes(w_{i1},x_{i1}^{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times(K+J_{1}+TK_{1})$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 Simply put, the vector of IVs for time period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ddot{x}_{it},w_{i1},x_{i1}^{o})$
\end_inset

.
 With this set of instruments, the order condition for identification of
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\gamma,\beta)$
\end_inset

 is that 
\begin_inset Formula $K+J_{1}+TK_{1}\geq J+K$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $TK_{1}\geq J_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 In effect, we must have a sufficient number of elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

 to act as instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 are the IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 act as their own IVs.) Whether we do depends on the number of time periods,
 as well as on 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Hausman and Taylor (1981) suggested estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by REIV.
 REIV can be implemented as a P2SLS estimator on transformed data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.6 Estimation under Sequential Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.6.1 General Framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also apply the FDIV methods in the previous section to estiamte the
 standard model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under a sequential exogeneity assumption, properly modified for the presence
 of the unobserved effect, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
sequential moment restriction
\series default
 can be written in conditional expectations form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{it},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,x_{i1},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When assumption (12) holds, we say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{it}\}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
sequentially exogenous conditional on the unobserved effect
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given equation (11), assumption (12) is equivalent to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{it},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,x_{i1},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which provides a simple interpretation of sequential exogeneity conditional
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

: once 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 have been controlled for, no 
\emph on
past
\emph default
 values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 affect the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Conditioning on the explanatory variables only up through time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is more natural than the strict exogeneity assumption, which requires condition
ing on future values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 as well.
 Models with lagged dependent variables are naturally analyzed under sequential
 exogeneity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A general approach to estimation under sequential exogeneity is taking first
 differences to remove 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and obtain equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{it}=\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

written in stacked form as in
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{i}=\Delta x_{i}\beta+\Delta u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The only issue is where the instruments come from.
 Under assumption (12),
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{is}u_{it})=0,\ s=1,\ldots,t;t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which implies the orthogonality conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{is}\Delta u_{it})=0,\ s=1,\ldots,t-1;t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the available instruments in the FD equation are in the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}^{o}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{it}^{o}\equiv(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{it})^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, the matrix of instruments is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W_{i}=\mbox{diag}(x_{i1}^{o\prime},\ldots,x_{i,T-1}^{o\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which has 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)$
\end_inset

 rows.
 Because of sequential exogeneity, the number of valid instruments increases
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

, it is routine to apply GMM estimation.
 However, some simpler strategies are available.
 One useful one is to estimate a reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 separately for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 So, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}^{o}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, and obtain the fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\Delta x}_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Of course, the fitted values are all 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vectors for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Then, estimate the FD equation (18) by PIV using instruments 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\Delta x}_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 It is simple to obtain robust standard errors and test statistics from
 such a procedure because the first state estimation to obtain the instruments
 can be ignored (asymptotically, of course).
 This is the same set of estimates obtained if we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

 as in (19) and choose as the weighting matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $(W^{\prime}W/N)^{-1}$
\end_inset

, that is, we obtain the S2SLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given an initial consistent estimator, we can obtain the efficient GMM weighting
 matrix.
 In most applications, there is a reasonable set of assumptions under which
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(W_{i}^{\prime}e_{i}e_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})=E(W_{i}^{\prime}\Omega W_{i})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=\Delta u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=E(e_{i}e_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, in which case the GMM 3SLS estimator is an efficient GMM estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.6.2 Models with Lagged Dependent Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A special case of models under sequential exogeneity restrictions are autoregres
sive models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=\alpha y_{i,t-1}+z_{it}^{\prime}\gamma+c_{i}+u_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 includes the regressors in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Due to the lagged dependent variable being used as a regressor, strict
 exogeneity is not satisfied.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with the future values of the regressors, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}x_{is})\ne0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $s=t+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we study the AR(1) model above.
 In the model without any other covariates,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{it}=\rho y_{i,t-1}+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T\\
E(u_{it}|y_{i,t-1},\ldots,y_{i0},c_{i})=0
\end{gather}

\end_inset

so that our first observation on 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption (21) says that we have the dynamics completely specified: once
 we control for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, only one lag of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 is necessary.
 This is an example of a 
\series bold
dynamic completeness conditional on the unobserved effect
\series default
 assumption.
 When we let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}=y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

, we see immediately that the AR(1) model satisfies the sequential exogeneity
 assumptions (conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One application of model (20) is to determine whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y_{it}\}$
\end_inset

 exhibits 
\series bold
state dependence
\series default
: after controlling for systemic, time-constant differences 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, does last period's outcome on 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 help predict this period's outcome.
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\rho=0$
\end_inset

 (non-predictability: the past dependent variable has no bearing on the
 current one).
 If we reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, then we should included a lagged dependent variable in the regressors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 in the FD equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y_{it}=\rho\Delta y_{i,t-1}+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, the avaiable instruments are 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}=(y_{i0},\ldots,y_{i,t-2})$
\end_inset

.
 Note, the differenced errors 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

 are necessarily correlated under assumption (21).
 Thus, at a minimum, any estimation method should account for this serial
 correlation in calculating standard errors and test statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A more general version of the model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=\theta_{t}+\rho y_{i,t-1}+z_{it}\gamma+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes different period intercepts and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{z_{it}\}$
\end_inset

 is strictly exogenous.
 When we difference equation (22),
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{it}=\eta_{t}+\rho\Delta y_{i,t-1}+\Delta z_{it}\gamma+\Delta u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the available instruments (in addition to time period dummies) are 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{i},y_{i,t-2},\ldots,y_{i0})$
\end_inset

.
 We might not want to use all of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for every period, but certaintly we would use 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta z_{it}$
\end_inset

 and maybe even 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta z_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 If we add sequentially exogenous variables, say 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{it}$
\end_inset

, to (22) then 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{i,t-1},\ldots,h_{i1})$
\end_inset

 would be added to the list.
 Remember, (32) is an estimating equation.
 We should interpret the estimates in light of the orginial model (22).
 It is equation (22) that represents a dynamically complete conditional
 expectation.
 Equation (23) does not even satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\Delta u_{it}|\Delta y_{i,t-1},\Delta z_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, which is why POLS estimation of (23) is inconsistent.
